export default "Cervical mucus is the most underused fertility sign despite being continuously accessible and requiring no equipment. Understanding how it changes across the cycle transforms it from random vaginal discharge into readable biological data.\n\n## The Full Cycle Pattern\n\nCervical mucus is produced by the cervix and changes in volume, texture, and clarity in response to estrogen and progesterone throughout the cycle.\n\n### Immediately After Menstruation: Dry or Minimal\n\nJust after your period ends, estrogen is at its lowest. Most people experience one to three dry days (no visible mucus at the vaginal opening), though some notice small amounts of white or yellowish mucus.\n\nWhat you observe: nothing, or thick white mucus that does not stretch. May look like lotion residue.\n\n### Early Follicular Phase: Sticky or Creamy\n\nAs estrogen begins rising, the cervix starts producing more mucus. Early follicular-phase mucus is typically:\n- White or pale yellow\n- Thick and pasty, or crumbly\n- Does not stretch significantly, breaks within 1 cm if stretched\n- Not slippery\n\nSometimes described as tacky or cement-like. This type is not considered fertile quality. Sperm cannot survive long in this environment.\n\n### Approaching Ovulation: Watery and Creamy\n\nAs estrogen continues rising, mucus volume increases and it becomes progressively more watery and creamy:\n- White or clear\n- Lotion-like consistency\n- Begins to have some stretch, 1 to 3 cm\n- Slightly lubricating\n\nThis transitional type signals increasing fertility but is not yet peak fertile quality.\n\n### Peak Fertile: Egg-White Cervical Mucus (EWCM)\n\nAt peak estrogen, typically one to three days before ovulation, cervical mucus reaches its most fertile form:\n- Clear to slightly opaque\n- Extremely slippery and lubricating (feels like touching lip balm or raw egg white)\n- Stretches 2 to 5 cm or more between fingers without breaking\n- Often abundant, may be noticeable on underwear or felt during movement\n\nThis is called egg-white cervical mucus (EWCM) for obvious reasons. It serves a functional purpose: the aligned glycoprotein fibers create channels that allow sperm to travel efficiently through the cervix. In non-fertile mucus, the random fiber arrangement blocks sperm movement.\n\nThe **peak day** is the last day of this type of mucus. You can only identify peak day looking backwards. When the mucus dries up or thickens, you know yesterday was peak day.\n\n### Post-Ovulation: Dry Again or Thick White\n\nAfter ovulation, progesterone takes over. Progesterone causes the cervix to produce thicker, denser mucus that forms a mucus plug blocking sperm entry. You may notice:\n- Quick return to thick, sticky, or dry mucus\n- White mucus that does not stretch\n- For some people, a sudden drying up\n\nThis pattern, wet and slippery before ovulation, abrupt change afterward, is the key observable shift that the Billings Ovulation Method uses to identify the fertile window.\n\n## How to Observe It Correctly\n\n**Where to check:** At the vaginal opening, using clean fingertips or by wiping with toilet paper before using the toilet. Do not insert fingers to collect internal mucus. The goal is to observe what is naturally present at the opening.\n\n**When to check:** Before urination (water dilutes and destroys mucus). Before sexual activity (arousal fluid is different from cervical mucus; it is thinner, more watery, and produced by vaginal walls rather than the cervix).\n\n**The stretch test:** Collect a small amount on your fingertip and touch it to your thumb. Slowly separate thumb and finger. Note: Does it stretch without breaking? How far? Does it break immediately?\n\n**Logging:** Track daily using a simple notation system:\n- D = dry (nothing visible)\n- S = sticky (does not stretch)\n- C = creamy (lotion-like, slight stretch)\n- EW = egg white (clear, stretchy, slippery)\n\n## Distinguishing Fertile Mucus from Vaginal Infections\n\nThis is clinically important and worth knowing:\n\n| Sign | Fertile Mucus | BV | Yeast Infection | Trichomoniasis |\n|------|----|----|----|-----|\n| Color | Clear to white | Gray-white | White | Yellow-green |\n| Consistency | Slippery, stretchy | Thin, watery | Thick, clumpy | Frothy |\n| Odor | None | Fishy (especially after sex) | None or yeasty | Foul |\n| Itching/burning | No | No/mild | Yes | Yes |\n| Timing | Mid-cycle | Anytime | Anytime | Anytime |\n\nFertile mucus is not accompanied by discomfort. If you have itching, burning, unusual odor, or significant color changes, get it evaluated.\n\n## Why Some People Do Not See EWCM\n\n**Antihistamines:** Dry up all mucus secretions, including cervical. Taking antihistamines during your fertile window will reduce or eliminate EWCM.\n\n**Dehydration:** Cervical fluid is partly hydration-dependent. Consistent water intake is particularly relevant in the pre-ovulatory window.\n\n**Clomiphene (Clomid):** This ovulation induction medication reduces cervical mucus quality as a side effect. It has anti-estrogenic effects at the cervix.\n\n**Low estrogen:** If estrogen does not rise adequately, fertile-quality mucus may not appear. This can be a sign of poor follicle development.\n\n**PCOS:** Irregular estrogen patterns in PCOS can produce irregular or absent fertile mucus patterns.\n\n**Some hormonal medications:** Certain medications beyond clomiphene affect mucus quality.\n\nNot observing EWCM is information, not failure. Combining LH strip testing with mucus observation gives you ovulation detection that does not depend entirely on mucus visibility.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nLogging daily mucus type alongside LH test results and basal body temperature creates the integrated picture of your cycle. A cycle where you see creamy mucus transitioning to egg-white, followed by a positive LH strip, followed by a BBT rise 24 to 36 hours later, is textbook ovulation confirmed from three independent signals. Deviations from this pattern, mucus but no LH surge, LH surge with no mucus, no BBT rise, point to specific aspects of cycle function worth investigating.";
